> [!proposal] **Reorder and Enhance DSL Grammar Section for Composability**  
> **Explanation:** The current DSL grammar is presented in a single block. I propose to start by framing the problem—introducing clear start (<START_POINT>) and endpoint (<END_POINT>) markers—and then breaking the grammar into logical components. This reordering will emphasize the compositional nature (focusing on "what does it do" rather than "what is it made of") and integrate examples that illustrate the transformation from an object-free arrow model to a full diagram.  
> **Sample Fragment:**  
> ```markdown
> ## DSL Grammar
> 
> <START_POINT>
> 
> diagram ::= { declaration }+
> 
> declaration ::= nodeDeclaration | edgeDeclaration | styleDeclaration
> 
> ... (other grammar rules)
> 
> <END_POINT>
> ```  
> **Status:** No Status

---

> [!proposal] **Enhance Example Section with Minimal and Extended Models**  
> **Explanation:** The examples should clearly illustrate how to build a diagram starting with minimal (object-free arrow model) components and then extending to full specifications. I propose updating the example to include both a minimal model and a detailed, composable example that demonstrates how to integrate nodes, edges, and style definitions effectively.  
> **Sample Fragment:**  
> ```markdown
> ## Example: Minimal Object-Free Arrow Model
> 
> ```d2,example
> # Minimal Diagram
> <START_POINT>
> arrow_model { /* minimal structure for arrow-based composition */ }
> <END_POINT>
> ```
> 
> ## Extended Example: Full Composable Diagram
> 
> ```d2,example
> # Extended Diagram with Nodes, Edges, and Styles
> local_repo: "Repository (Toy Box)";
> tracking: "Tracking Branch (origin/main Note)";
> remote_repo: "Remote Repository (GitHub Mirror)";
> 
> local_repo -> tracking { label: "updates note" };
> tracking -> remote_repo { label: "points to" };
> 
> style local_repo {
>   icon: "assets/toy_box.svg";
>   fill: "#F4F4F4";
>   stroke: "#333";
>   stroke-width: 1;
> }
> ```
> ```  
